From a352bf4b64b9356499404d05d4d6e5aa1ec6da0a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cheng-Yang Chou <yphbchou0911@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 09:50:26 +0800
Subject: [PATCH sched_ext/for-7.5 1/1] selftests/sched_ext: Test that the
 kfunc declarations in the scx headers bind
To: Tejun Heo <tj@kernel.org>
Cc: David Vernet <void@manifault.com>,
    Andrea Righi <arighi@nvidia.com>,
    Changwoo Min <changwoo@igalia.com>,
    Emil Tsalapatis <emil@etsalapatis.com>,
    fangqiurong <fangqiurong@kylinos.cn>,
    sched-ext@lists.linux.dev,
    linux-kernel@vger.kernel.org

A weak kfunc extern that libbpf can't bind stays NULL without an error,
and the compat wrapper using it silently falls back to an older kfunc.
Two such declarations went out in v7.1 and stayed unnoticed for two
releases: scx_bpf_dsq_reenq___compat declared the implicit aux argument,
and scx_bpf_dsq_move_to_local___v2 resolved to
scx_bpf_dsq_move_to_local, as libbpf strips the last ___ suffix. They
were fixed by:

  commit 62f3d531e41a ("sched_ext: Fix scx_bpf_dsq_reenq___compat kfunc extern prototype")
  commit 56bbc91219c0 ("sched_ext: Fix scx_bpf_dsq_move_to_local___v2 compat detection")

Add a test that records which of the weak kfunc declarations in
common.bpf.h, and of the newest variants in compat.bpf.h, libbpf bound.
It fails if one didn't bind although the kernel has the function libbpf
resolves it to. With either fix reverted, it fails with:

  scx_bpf_dsq_reenq___compat didn't bind to scx_bpf_dsq_reenq
  scx_bpf_dsq_move_to_local___v2 didn't bind to scx_bpf_dsq_move_to_local

Signed-off-by: Cheng-Yang Chou <yphbchou0911@gmail.com>
